            <ArticleTitle>[Comparative evaluation of the efficacy of nitrosorbide and nitroglycerin in acute myocardial infarct patients].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>41-6</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Intravenous isosorbide dinitrate and nitroglycerin administered within early hours of myocardial infarction have a marked antianginal effect, stabilize the size of necrotic focus and promptly control acute left-ventricular insufficiency. Unlike nitroglycerin, isosorbide dinitrate has a more lasting action and does not affect heart rate and arterial blood pressure. It can therefore be used more extensively in patients with acute myocardial infarction.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
